## Solver Notes

### Bruteforce

- row-major empty-cell order
- tries values `1..N`
- local consistency checks only
- slow baseline

### Backtracking

- main reference solver
- MRV
- degree heuristic
- forward checking
- AC-3

### A*

- state is propagated domains, not only a raw grid
- `g(s)` is the number of branching decisions
- `h0(s)=0`
- `hweak(s)=0 or 1`
- `hmain(s)` is the number of ambiguous connected constraint components after AC-3

Why `hmain` is admissible:

- after AC-3 reaches a fixed point, each still-ambiguous connected component has no determined value for at least one cell
- one action assigns one cell, and disconnected binary-constraint components do not propagate into each other
- therefore each ambiguous component needs at least one future decision under this state/action definition

### Logic Forward

- real ground Horn KB
- real agenda-based forward chaining
- derives `Val`, `NotVal`, `Possible`, `Assigned`
- clearly marked fallback to search if inference alone is incomplete

### Logic Backward

- real SLD-style backward chaining engine
- solver uses SLD queries over a propagated domain snapshot, then clearly separates fallback search
- clearly marked fallback to search if inference alone is incomplete

## Verify and CNF

- `src/futoshiki/logic/cnf.py` contains both a symbolic CNF pipeline and a direct propositional encoder
- `verify` checks output inequality signs, strict solution validation, and CNF satisfaction
- SAT/CNF is verification-only, not the main solver

## UI

The Streamlit app provides:

- bundled input selection or file upload
- text editor in assignment input format
- board rendering with `<`, `>`, `^`, `v`
- solver selection
- run, run all, validate, reset, export output
- metrics and logs
- benchmark tab
- logic query panel
- CNF statistics panel
- theory tab

## Benchmark

Files:

- [reports/benchmark_results.csv](/C:/MEGA/co%20so%20ttnt/Project%202/reports/benchmark_results.csv)
- [reports/benchmark_summary.md](/C:/MEGA/co%20so%20ttnt/Project%202/reports/benchmark_summary.md)

Current bundled benchmark summary:

- `backtracking` is the best overall reference solver in this codebase
- `logic-forward` is the slowest because the grounded Horn rule base is large
- `logic-backward` is faster than `logic-forward`
- `astar-main` and `astar-h0` both solve the full bundled dataset

## Tests

```bash
python -m pytest -q
```

Current status: `20 passed`

## Academic Honesty

- `logic-forward` and `logic-backward` are not fake labels for a CSP solver
- both logic solvers keep their intended inference behavior
- when they need fallback search, that is stated explicitly in code, logs, README, and report
- `verify_sat.py` is not used as the main solver

